    "proof": "Assume the conclusion fails and choose a sequence of counterexamples with $N\\to\\infty$. By @def-admissible-polygon, the canonical presentation consists of effective edges; by @lem-edge-moments, stationarity supplies the edge moments. Thus @lem-near-circle-conformal applies and gives $S\\le CN^{-3}$. For all sufficiently large indices this is below the fixed smallness thresholds in @lem-disk-perturbation and @lem-gradient-perturbation, so the hypotheses of @lem-spectral-core, @lem-two-reflex-exclusion, @lem-single-reflex-small-atom, and @lem-atom-deletion are met.\n\nIf there are at least two reflex corners, @lem-two-reflex-exclusion gives a contradiction. Thus, after discarding finitely many terms, there is exactly one reflex corner. The hypotheses of @lem-single-reflex-small-atom and @lem-atom-deletion hold, so the deleted measure has $N-2$ positive atoms and @lem-kernel-comparison yields $E_b(\\widetilde\\mu)\\ge B_{N-2}$. By @lem-atom-deletion,\n$$\nE_b(\\mu)\\ge B_{N-2}-o(N^{-4}).\n$$\nThe spectral-core estimate @lem-spectral-core together with @def-turn-measure and @lem-kernel-comparison gives\n$$\n\\frac{J(P)}{\\pi j^2}\\ge1+4E_b(\\mu)-C S^{3/2}\\sqrt{\\log(e/S)}.\n$$\nUsing @lem-near-circle-conformal, the error is $o(N^{-4})$, so\n$$\n\\frac{J(P)}{\\pi j^2}\\ge1+4B_{N-2}-o(N^{-4}).\n$$\nThe regular-polygon asymptotic @lem-regular-polygon-asymptotic gives\n$$\n\\frac{J(R_{N-1})}{\\pi j^2}=1+\\frac{4\\zeta(3)}{(N-1)^3}+O(N^{-9/2}),\n$$\nwhile @lem-bp-asymptotic gives $B_{N-2}=\\zeta(3)(N-2)^{-3}+O(N^{-5})$. Their difference is $12\\zeta(3)N^{-4}+O(N^{-9/2})+o(N^{-4})>0$ for large $N$, contradicting $J(P)\\le J(R_{N-1})$.\n\nFinally, by @def-j-functional, $m_{N-1}$ is the infimum over admissible polygons with at most $N-1$ effective edges, and $R_{N-1}$ is one such polygon; hence $m_{N-1}\\le J(R_{N-1})$. Therefore any nonconvex stationary polygon with $J(P)<m_{N-1}$ also satisfies the threshold already ruled out.",
